{"id": "T.worker.1", "text": "Whether m[i, j] := v should reach a subscripted assignment opr[i: I] := (v: E) at a pair index, as m[i, j] reaches opr[i: I] (P-156: 'm[i, j] passes its tuple (i, j) as one index'). Both paths read the assignment's arguments as one list, value then subscripts: walk 'The number of parameters (2) does not match with the number of arguments (3)'; fortress compile 'Could not check method invocation Cells._[_]:= - (ZZ32, (ZZ32, ZZ32))->() is not applicable to an argument of type (NaturalNumeral[\\1,10,7\\], NaturalNumeral[\\1,10,5\\], NaturalNumeral[\\1,10,6\\])', for Cells[5, 6] := 7 against opr[t: (ZZ32, ZZ32)] := (e: ZZ32). The one library declares opr[x:ZZ32,y:ZZ32]:=(v:T) beside the form over I for this (Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2860, :3286); Specification/advanced/subscripting.tex, section 'Subscripted Assignment Operator Method Declarations', does not say how the bracketed list meets one tuple-typed parameter. Decision not taken; nothing changed.", "inPlan": true}
{"id": "T.skeptic.1", "text": "The worker asked whether m[i, j] := v should reach a subscripted assignment opr[i: I] := (v: E) at a pair index, as m[i, j] reaches opr[i: I] (P-156). I reproduced its evidence. Under walk, Cells[5, 6] := 7 against opr[t: (ZZ32, ZZ32)] := (e: ZZ32) gives '** bug! The number of parameters (2) does not match with the number of arguments (3).'. fortress compile gives '(ZZ32, (ZZ32, ZZ32))->() is not applicable to an argument of type (NaturalNumeral[\\1,10,7\\], NaturalNumeral[\\1,10,5\\], NaturalNumeral[\\1,10,6\\])' (tmp program VSub.fss). The one library declares the element form beside the form over I: Library/FortressLibrary.fss:2860 'opr[x:ZZ32,y:ZZ32]:=(v:T):() = do self[ (x,y) ] := v end' and :3286. Specification/advanced/subscripting.tex, section 'Subscripted Assignment Operator Method Declarations', does not say. No decision was taken.", "inPlan": true}
{"id": "O.worker.1", "text": "The specification presents ZZ as a trait (Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex:16, :106, :229), as it presents Boolean (Specification/basic-lib/booleans.tex:80), which the library has declared as a value object; with P-178 the one library declares ZZ, ZZ32, ZZ64, NN64 and RR64 as value objects (Library/FortressLibrary.fss:384, :846, :951, :1063, :1170). Whether the chapter's text should say object, or keep the api style, is open; the rung left it.", "inPlan": true}
{"id": "O.worker.2", "text": "The outputs of the 163 expected-failure walk tests were not compared between the old code and the new: the automatic permission check refused the base build's harness-one.sh run with FORTRESS_HOME set to the base build ('Destructive or Unwanted Actions'), the form of worktrees.md, 'Running the old code'. All 163 pass on the edit (tmp run, 'OK (163 tests)'), but whether one's expected failure moved from run time to a refusal at load is not established. The way through is a manual approval or an allow rule, or the gate's skeptic running it.", "inPlan": true}
{"id": "O.worker.3", "text": "The brief said to leave compiler_tests/XXXNativeHelperOwnUnsignedLongCompiled.fss, while its step 4 removes the rewriter's UnsignedLong clause (ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/BuiltinPrimitiveRewriter.java:189); with the clause gone the test's pinned message changes, so its key and comment were repinned (ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXNativeHelperOwnUnsignedLongCompiled.test:3). A reversible point for review.", "inPlan": true}
{"id": "O.skeptic.1", "text": "Whether the specification's library chapter should present ZZ, ZZ32, ZZ64, NN64 and RR64 as objects, or keep its api style of a trait. It presents ZZ as a trait (Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex:16, :106, :229), as it presents Boolean (Specification/basic-lib/booleans.tex:80), though the library declares Boolean a value object. The one library now declares the five as value objects (Library/FortressLibrary.fss:384, :846, :951, :1063, :1170). The worker's question, still open.", "inPlan": true}
{"id": "O.skeptic.2", "text": "The brief told the worker to leave ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXNativeHelperOwnUnsignedLongCompiled.fss alone, while step 4 removes the rewriter's UnsignedLong clause (ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/compiler/BuiltinPrimitiveRewriter.java:189). With the clause gone, the test's pinned message changes, so its key was repinned (ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXNativeHelperOwnUnsignedLongCompiled.test:3). This is a reversible point for review; the skeptic agrees with the repin.", "inPlan": true}
